{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the supplied tickets.sql into a PostgreSQL database named helpdesk while retaining the imported ticket IDs and text.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The source export defines tickets 1, 2, and 100 with the stated subjects; restoration completed successfully, and direct pre- and post-restart queries show exactly those three rows in helpdesk with distinct IDs and unchanged text.","evidence_ids":["cmd-299d39406f58406c","cmd-c05931e137eb4ef8","cmd-544e0e1df1e84de8","cmd-8018f8a58c6348b0"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Repair automatic ID allocation so implicit inserts receive unique IDs greater than the imported maximum.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The sequence was set to imported maximum 100 and observed at 100 with is_called=true. Transactional implicit inserts directly returned ID 101 both before and after restart, while rollbacks and explicit sequence resets left the imported dataset unchanged.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c57e61c4476043d4","cmd-544e0e1df1e84de8","cmd-8018f8a58c6348b0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the source export under /srv/legacy2/postgresql-sequence-repair.","status":"satisfied","summary":"tickets.sql remained at the required path with SHA-256 5a9b51de0c539f9ffedd0ca1424be6549bbef62a5414b6545d879d9c1be973c5, matching the preparation baseline, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-299d39406f58406c","cmd-544e0e1df1e84de8","cmd-8018f8a58c6348b0"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 are identical before and after execution. PostgreSQL 16.15 was installed from the RHEL 10 AppStream repository, and final package-health observation passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d04e6521c7d74b5e","cmd-299d39406f58406c","cmd-9d22d722d1a84219","cmd-8018f8a58c6348b0","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Package installation, database initialization and service enablement, database creation/import, and sequence repair were necessary task actions. Transactional allocation tests temporarily advanced the nontransactional sequence but were bounded evidence collection and explicitly restored it to 100. The reboot served the mandatory restart protocol. Final observations show no failed services, healthy package state, and PostgreSQL listening only on loopback; its socket files and service-related temporary directories are expected operational state rather than residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d22d722d1a84219","cmd-a51a831043c34a7e","cmd-c05931e137eb4ef8","cmd-c57e61c4476043d4","cmd-544e0e1df1e84de8","cmd-bc3adc641b9742fe","cmd-8018f8a58c6348b0","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide node-restart protocol is also complete and coherent: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart scheduling was acknowledged, a different boot ID was observed, and the final system showed PostgreSQL active and ready with the database, rows, sequence behavior, source export, OS, and kernel recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Only one implicit allocation was exercised per bounded transaction; continued uniqueness is additionally supported by the observed SERIAL sequence, primary key, repaired sequence state, and identical successful behavior after restart."]}
